this video is sponsored by longevity  technology our results reveal that the  microbiome may be a suitable therapeutic  target to promote healthy aging  so hello and welcome to the cheeky  science show where in this video we are  going to discuss the gut microbiome and  its connection to brain aging so by the  end of this video you will understand  what some scientists have done to  improve the brain health of some old  mice and what this all has to do with  the gut microbiome so firstly what  actually is the gut microbiome well it  refers to the collection of all the  different microorganisms such as  bacteria fungi and viruses that inhabit  our gut  and our microbiomes aren't just  passengers hanging around in our body  they actually perform a variety of  different functions such as helping with  the digestion of foods but beyond that  the gut microbiome has also been linked  with mediating various processes  including shaping our immune response  and so unsurprisingly dysbiosis or  fargott microbiome has also been linked  to several different diseases  in particular it's been associated with  obesity and type 2 diabetes  so it may not come as surprised as well  that the microbiome has been shown to  change with age and there's actually  been a study that's shown that you can  use the microbiome to be able to predict  someone's biological age which is pretty  cool and so because of these connections  a lot of research has been investigated  in understanding the gut microbiome and  some more recent markers shown that it's  also involved in metabolism and brain  health  and so given that the gut microbiome  undergoes many changes during aging if  there was a way to counteract these  changes would it also have improvements  on these different diseases well this is  kind of a big question and to kind of  address it you need to study it in modal  organisms first and focus on more  specific outcomes and so this brings us  on to this recent nature aging  publication i wanted to see if the  microbiota from young mice could help to  rejuvenate  signs of aging in the brain of odomites  and so the lovely way that you can test  this is through a process known as fecal  microbiota transplantation and so i was  going to explain exactly how this  procedure works but having read the  methods section of this paper i feel  like we'll save that for another day and  simply look at this delightful figure  that shows broadly what happens  so anyway now that we understand the  procedure what they did was they took  the fecal microbiota from young mice  that were aged around three to four  months and transplanted this into aged  mites that were around 19 to 20 months  and as a control they also gave the aged  mice fecal microbiota from older mice  and they also gave young mice microbiota  from young mice  and these transplantations were  continued throughout the rest of the  study and as you can see here they did a  variety of different measurements and  these range from assessing brain  immunity to looking at more complex  behavioral functions but firstly did the  microbiota composition even change  well to some extent yes and the case  pointed out by the authors where changes  seen in the enterococcus general  whereby you can see that the age mice  that were given the young microbiator to  a greater abundance more closely  resembling what's seen in the younger  mice  and that's interesting given the fact  that some enterococcus species are  already being used as probiotics to  improve animal health  but did any of these changes actually  reflect an improvement in immune  function or brain health  well to assess brain health they looked  at the hippocampus of these mice which  is a small region of the brain involved  in learning and memory  and given that the microbiome generates  a load of different chemicals that they  secrete into the body they first assess  the hippocampal metabolome and so this  figure shows how there's many different  changes in the metabolites in age mice  that were given the young fecal  microbiota compared to given old  microbiota  and 35 of the metabolites that were  altered in aging were restored towards  pre-age levels by the introduction of  this young fecal microbiota and of these  metabolites that already have no new  connections between neurotransmission  and aging the authors identified retinol  or vitamin a  gamma-aminobutyric acid known as gaba  and  glycolyranamate and also arginine and  other metabolites were enriched in  pathways involved in amino acid  metabolism and trna biosynthesis  and so this is all interesting because a  lot of these metabolites are linked with  proper brain function for example a  previous study has shown that vitamin a  supplementation prevents age-related  spatial memory deficits and arginine has  been linked to neurodegeneration  and so they saw some restorative changes  in the metabolism in the hippocampus of  these aged mice given young fecal  microbiota but that doesn't have any  functional consequences  well as i mentioned in the study they  also assessed a variety of different  behavioral tests with the mice given  young fecal microbiota and one of these  tests that they did included the morris  water maze which is a way of determining  hippocampal dependent long-term spatial  memory and so typically hmi show  impairments in the ability to perform  this task but this was somewhat improved  in the hmis that had received the young  microbiota now one reason why this might  have happened is because there's  improved neurogenesis the development of  new neuronal cells however there was no  evidence to support increased  neurogenesis in these mice  and so it's more likely that these  changes in metabolites are having the  influence seen in their behavior however  this requires further investigation  and so the last aspect they looked at  was whether or not the gut microbiota  from the young mice could have any  influence over host immunity in the old  mice and so the immune cells of the  brain are the microglial and typically  during aging it's observed that there's  enlargement of these microglia which  reflects increased activation of the  microglia that's also been associated  with neurodegenerative conditions  and so the old mice that were given the  young fecal microbiota showed a  reduction in the size of their microglia  suggesting that the fecal microbiota  transfer  could be modulating hippocampal immunity  however the transfer didn't reverse  age-associated reductions in interferon  gamma or interleukin-5 suggesting that  the transfer may be selectively  modulating peripheral immunity and so  this work raises a number of questions  the major one being what is the  mechanism behind these differences  whilst they saw changes in the  metabolism in the hippocampus is that  because the microbiome were producing  these compounds or were they synthesized  within the brain  and could those metabolites alone or in  combination refers to morphological and  functional alterations in the aging  hippocampus as opposed to actually  having the fecal microbiome transfer  particularly because well  there may be some reasons why people  would object to such a treatment but  also due to the fact that in this paper  the mice had to have continued treatment  throughout the whole time  and so that raises the second question  as well how long do these effects last  is it like a one-time thing and then  they'll be fine for the remainder or how  frequent would these treatments need to  be for the mice to show these continued  improvements  another interesting point to raise is  the fact that the fecal microbiome  transfers only take the microbiome from  well the fecal material and that  actually doesn't necessarily reflect the  whole gut microbiome profile of the  young mice and so it'd be interesting to  know if actually having a better  representation of the young gut  microbiome  in the older mice would maybe show more  benefits  and speaking more just on the method  itself it's worth pointing out that  heterologous fecal microbiome transfer  is a highly successful and  cost-effective therapy for the treatment  of recurrent see difficile infection  however another concern with doing these  treatments is the long-term safety of  the procedure and potential exposure to  life-threatening infectious diseases an  alternative is autologous fmt therapy so  that would be banking your own stool  samples when healthy to later rebalance  your gut microbiome  which um i'll let you have your own  opinions on that  so with that i'd like to thank the  sponsor for this week's video longevity  technology for which i'm very grateful  longevity technology delivers high  quality daily news and insights on  research investments and technologies  that extend health span and lifespan  find the link to their website in the  description  so i hope you've learnt something in  this video thank you to my patreon  supporters and thank you for listening

